1
00:00:01,500 --> 00:00:11,440
I’d been living in Los Angeles just under a year when, in the spring of 1983, I answered an ad in the Hollywood Reporter for a receptionist and got the job.
我在洛杉矶住了还不到一年，1983年春天，应征了《好莱坞报道者》上一则招聘接待员的广告，得到了这份工作。

2
00:00:12,240 --> 00:00:19,370
The pay wasn’t much, but the work was in “the Business”—an apt Los Angeles euphemism for the entertainment industry.
工资不高，但干的是“那一行”的活儿——在洛杉矶，这是对娱乐业的一种贴切的委婉说法。

3
00:00:20,170 --> 00:00:26,010
The location was within bicycling distance of my home, and they only wanted someone to work mornings.
上班地点离我家不远，骑自行车就能到，而且他们要找的人只需每天上午来上班。

4
00:00:26,810 --> 00:00:31,710
I’d get off by 1 p.m., which I thought would leave me plenty of time to do my own writing.
我下午1点前就能下班，原以为这样能给我留出大把时间，写自己的东西。

5
00:00:32,510 --> 00:00:33,860
I was wrong about that.
这一点我想错了。

6
00:00:34,660 --> 00:00:40,080
The place was so frantic I’d come out wired and need the rest of the day to simply calm down.
那地方忙乱得厉害，我每次下班出来都神经紧绷，光是让自己平静下来，就得花掉一天里剩下的全部时间。

7
00:00:40,880 --> 00:00:51,710
When, after about two weeks, I realized my afternoons were being spent in activities equivalent to running around the block twenty or thirty times, I asked to be put on full-time.
大约两周后，我意识到自己的下午都耗在了一些相当于绕着街区跑二三十圈的事情上，于是要求转为全职。

8
00:00:52,510 --> 00:00:59,190
Since I wasn’t getting anything else done, why not, I figured, jump in for the total experience?
既然别的事情我一件也没做成，我心想，何不索性一头扎进去，好好体验个彻底？

9
00:00:59,990 --> 00:01:07,460
The (still thriving) company I found myself a part of possessed the unlikely name of Breakdown Services, Ltd.
我就这样成了这家公司（至今仍生意兴隆）的一员，它有个出人意料的名字：Breakdown Services有限公司。

10
00:01:08,260 --> 00:01:20,520
During the six months I worked there, I learned to savor all of that phrase’s more cynical reverberations, but in terms of the job, it dealt with the dismantling or breakdown of television and film scripts.
在那里工作的六个月里，我学会了细细品味这个名称中所有更带讥讽意味的弦外之音；不过就工作而言，它涉及的是拆解或者说分解影视剧本。

11
00:01:21,320 --> 00:01:33,710
Scripts gathered from producers or studios would be regurgitated by Breakdown in the form of plot summaries, character descriptions, the number of scenes per character, and the number of dialogue lines each speaks.
Breakdown公司会把从制片人或制片厂那里收集来的剧本重新“吐”出来，变成剧情梗概、人物描述、每个角色的出场场数以及各自的台词句数。

12
00:01:34,510 --> 00:01:43,380
These compilations were then xeroxed and distributed to hundreds of subscribing actors’ agents who proceeded to submit their clients for likely parts.
这些汇编材料随后被复印出来，分发给数百名订阅了分解表的演员经纪人，经纪人们接着便为自己的客户报名，争取那些可能合适的角色。

13
00:01:44,180 --> 00:01:55,990
It was a process, as I was to discover by way of angry phone calls when “breakdowns” arrived late or not at all, that the agents regarded with a reverence others might reserve for morning prayer.
每当“分解表”送迟了或者根本没送到，愤怒的电话就会打来，我后来正是通过这些电话发现，经纪人们对这道流程怀着一份虔敬，而别人或许只会把这样的虔敬留给晨祷。

14
00:01:56,790 --> 00:02:02,040
Breakdown Services, Ltd., was the brainchild of a young man named Gary Marsh.
Breakdown Services有限公司是一个名叫加里·马什的年轻人的独创。

15
00:02:02,840 --> 00:02:11,030
Gary was twenty-eight when I met him, but he’d founded Breakdown when he was eighteen and was considered something of an entrepreneurial prodigy.
我认识加里时，他二十八岁，但他十八岁就创办了Breakdown公司，人们认为他算得上是个创业神童。

16
00:02:11,830 --> 00:02:21,430
His mother was an agent and he’d observed the piles of scripts she and her agent pals had to plow through in order to match a given role with the talent they represented.
他的母亲是一名经纪人，他曾看到，为了把某个特定角色和自己所代理的艺人对上号，母亲和她那些当经纪人的朋友不得不埋头啃完一摞摞剧本。

17
00:02:22,230 --> 00:02:30,760
He’d also observed how much they hated the reading part of their jobs and had cleverly devised breakdowns as a way of extracting the material they needed.
他还看出他们有多讨厌工作中要阅读的那一部分，便巧妙地设计出分解表，用这种办法来提取他们所需的材料。

18
00:02:31,560 --> 00:02:38,520
By the time I arrived, five full-time breakdown writers worked in a desk-lined back room overlooking a parking lot.
我来的时候，已有五名全职的分解表写手在一间后屋里工作，屋里排满了办公桌，窗外是一片停车场。

19
00:02:39,320 --> 00:02:45,060
I’ve heard the current crew has now advanced to computers, but when I was there typewriters were the norm.
我听说如今那里的工作人员已经进步到用电脑了，而我在那儿的时候，打字机还是常规配置。

20
00:02:45,860 --> 00:02:58,960
When they weren’t typing, the writers would curl up on the office’s worn stuffed couch and, with a script propped against one arm, fill legal-sized yellow pads with dialogue line numbers and comments which they’d then type up.
写手们不打字的时候，会蜷在办公室那张磨旧了的软垫长沙发上，把剧本支在一侧扶手上，在一本本法律文书尺寸的黄色拍纸簿上写满台词句数和评语，然后再把这些内容用打字机打出来。

21
00:02:59,760 --> 00:03:01,380
It could be a complex job.
这项工作有时会很复杂。

22
00:03:02,180 --> 00:03:14,580
For example, the breakdown for a film originally called Teenage Gambler lists over forty characters, ranging from the teenaged gambling leader to five waiters who sing “Happy Birthday” at a surprise party.
例如，有一部电影原名《少年赌徒》，它的分解表列出了四十多个角色，从十几岁的赌博头目，一直到在惊喜派对上唱《祝你生日快乐》的五名服务员。

23
00:03:15,380 --> 00:03:24,930
Perhaps because of the patience involved in this sort of extraction, women seemed to dominate the ranks of the break-downers (during my tenure the ratio was four to one).
或许是因为这类提取工作需要耐心，分解表写手的队伍似乎以女性为主（我在职期间，比例是四比一）。

24
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:34,110
Although Gary knew I’d done a bit of writing, I was relegated to the front office and never received an invitation to try my hand at breakdowns.
虽然加里知道我写过一点东西，我却被打发到了前台办公室，也从没受邀去试写分解表。

25
00:03:34,910 --> 00:03:37,180
It wasn’t something I really wanted to do.
那并不是我真心想做的事。

26
00:03:37,980 --> 00:03:41,010
For one thing, the writers were a youthful bunch.
一来，那些写手都是一帮年轻人。

27
00:03:41,810 --> 00:03:46,850
The back room senior was in her early thirties, but none of the others had hit twenty-five.
后屋里最年长的那位三十出头，而其他人没有一个满二十五岁。

28
00:03:47,650 --> 00:03:53,930
And I think Gary rightly surmised I’d be too openly cynical for the job or try to embellish too much.
而且我认为，加里猜得没错：我要么会冷嘲热讽得太露骨，不适合这份工作，要么会试图过多地添枝加叶。

29
00:03:54,730 --> 00:04:00,110
Breakdowns were produced anonymously and offered no artistic evaluations of the scripts.
分解表都是匿名制作的，对剧本也不作任何艺术上的评价。

30
00:04:00,910 --> 00:04:11,170
The writers might talk among themselves about a piece they felt was particularly good or bad, but such editorializing was not allowed to make its way into the final product.
写手们私下里可能会谈论某部他们觉得特别好或特别糟的作品，但这类评头论足是不允许混进最终成品的。

31
00:04:11,970 --> 00:04:21,710
That this restriction was frustrating is indicated by the fact that two of the writers began venting opinions as moonlighting theater critics for small local papers.
这项限制让人憋闷，这一点从下面这个事实就能看出来：其中两名写手开始给当地的小型报纸兼职当剧评人，借此一抒己见。

32
00:04:22,510 --> 00:04:28,150
For Gary, the most problematical aspect of the breakdown business was its limited market.
对加里来说，分解表这门生意最成问题的一点是市场有限。

33
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:34,940
This had nothing to do with his operating methodology; he did what he could to exploit the possibilities.
这和他的经营方法毫无关系；他已经尽其所能去挖掘各种可能性。

34
00:04:35,740 --> 00:04:49,450
Each weekday a hardy crew made predawn deliveries of breakdowns to agents’ homes or offices, while a post-dawn quartet of pager-equipped Breakdown field workers haunted the big studios ready to pounce on an available script.
每个工作日，一班吃苦耐劳的人员在天亮前把分解表送到经纪人的家里或办公室；天亮之后，由四名配有寻呼机的Breakdown外勤人员组成的小队则频频出没于各大制片厂，随时准备扑向能弄到手的剧本。

35
00:04:50,250 --> 00:05:08,490
For these studio prowlers speed was of the essence, not only because Hollywood tends to be crisis-prone and wants everything done quickly, but because a rival, spawned by the success of Breakdown Services, Ltd., and infuriatingly named BreakThrough Productions, Ltd., was also on the hunt.
对这些在制片厂里四处转悠的搜寻者来说，速度至关重要，这不仅是因为好莱坞往往容易出现危机，凡事都要求快速办妥，还因为有一个竞争对手也在四处搜寻：这家公司是Breakdown Services有限公司的成功催生出来的，名字起得让人恼火，叫BreakThrough Productions有限公司。

36
00:05:09,290 --> 00:05:15,650
Breakdown Services unquestionably dominated the field, but the field itself had immutable perimeters.
Breakdown Services公司无疑在这一领域占据主导地位，但这个领域本身有着不可改变的边界。

37
00:05:16,450 --> 00:05:21,730
Breakdown’s subscribers had to be accredited agents, though there were some exceptions to this rule.
Breakdown公司的订户必须是获得正式认可的经纪人，不过这条规定也有一些例外。

38
00:05:22,530 --> 00:05:35,410
For instance, specialized media organizations, such as the competitive, vulture-like companies that insured movie productions, could keep tabs on the industry by subscribing to the weekly Breakdown summaries.
例如，专门的媒体机构——诸如那些为电影摄制提供保险、争强好胜、像秃鹫一样的公司——就可以通过订阅Breakdown公司每周的摘要，密切关注这个行业。

39
00:05:36,210 --> 00:05:39,280
But the whole Breakdown operation was hard to monitor.
但整个Breakdown业务很难监控。

40
00:05:40,080 --> 00:05:46,260
When I was there, a Breakdown subscription was expensive, something like $500 a year.
我在那儿的时候，订阅Breakdown的费用很高，一年大约500美元。

41
00:05:47,060 --> 00:06:02,080
And though breakdowns were copyrighted and unauthorized reproduction was clearly prohibited, xerox machines are notoriously bad at picking up such distinctions, and among the larger agencies breakdowns were undoubtedly duplicated and passed around.
而且，尽管分解表受版权保护，未经授权的复制也是明令禁止的，但复印机出了名地不善于分辨这类区别；在那些较大的经纪公司里，分解表无疑被复印出来，四处传阅。

42
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:07,370
Gary’s response to these built-in economic dilemmas was to diversify.
加里应对这些固有经济困境的办法是多元化经营。

43
00:06:08,170 --> 00:06:13,880
A separate department for commercials, for example, appeared as part of the Breakdown menu.
例如，一个专做广告片的独立部门出现了，成为Breakdown业务菜单的一部分。

44
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:32,350
Although Breakdown staffers were supposed to refrain from giving tips to potential talent, I did once tell a friend—who hoped to finance the college educations of her five-year-old identical twins by getting them on a commercial—that a juice company had put out a call for identical twin girls.
虽然Breakdown的员工按规定应避免向有望成为艺人的人透露内部消息，但我确实有一次告诉一位朋友，说有家果汁公司在公开征召同卵双胞胎女孩。这位朋友希望让她五岁的同卵双胞胎拍上广告，以此为她们上大学筹钱。

45
00:06:33,150 --> 00:06:36,440
Their agent submitted them, but they didn’t get the job.
她们的经纪人给她们报了名，但她们没被选上。

46
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:42,030
The nightly Breakdown delivery system expanded to an all-day messenger service.
Breakdown每晚的递送系统扩展成了全天的专人送件服务。

47
00:06:42,830 --> 00:06:52,720
Gary also made available an assortment of directories and mailing labels listing casting directors, talent agents, and literary agents in Los Angeles and New York.
加里还提供各式各样的名录和邮寄标签，上面列有洛杉矶和纽约的选角导演、演艺经纪人和文学经纪人。

48
00:06:53,520 --> 00:07:06,390
The current Breakdown brochure adds yet another Breakdown amenity; for fifteen dollars you can receive an “actors’ relaxation” cassette tape “designed to maximize your abilities and stimulate your creative senses”.
如今的Breakdown宣传册上又添了一项Breakdown便利服务：花十五美元，你就能得到一盒“演员放松”磁带，它“旨在最大限度地发挥你的能力，激发你的创造性感官”。
